  "configuration_id": "config_018",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_001 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) achieved 0.485 acc, while config_016 (multimodal, singleprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, concat) improved to 0.527 acc / 0.510 F1, confirming that adding the image modality with concat fusion is beneficial. However, the multiprofile axis remains completely unexplored. Question: Does using multiple profiles (with simple avgpool aggregation) on top of the validated multimodal setup (pca_n2000 + resnet50 + concat) further improve performance? Why this is best: config_018 isolates the multiprofile effect by keeping all other settings identical to the best-known configuration (config_016) and using the simplest aggregation (avgpool), so any performance change can be attributed to the multi-profile design. This is a high-impact design decision—if multiprofile helps, it opens a large sub-space of configurations; if it doesn't, we can focus the remaining budget on singleprofile variants (fusion method, encoder choices). With only 2 experiments completed, resolving this major axis is the most information-efficient next step."
}

